es: count check failures regardless.\n\nDesign of src/routing.rs:\n\n```rust\n//! Purpose: dry-run fault routing — proposals for tool-fault tickets in the tool's repo.\n//! Responsibilities: filter tier-2 report rows' failing checks to tool faults (no `code`), tally distinct models per fault key, apply the min(3,|registry|) routing threshold, and emit proposal rows with the fingerprint token and full machine-checkable schema.\n//! Rationale: openspec add-fault-ticket-routing — the fault taxonomy routes tool faults to tickets; dry-run default keeps cross-repo writes behind an explicit opt-in (evallerina-2bz), and the schema makes the CI artifact reviewable without reading code.\n```\n\nStruct ProposalRow (serialize to JSON):\n- tool (recorded tool — repo keying), repo: Option<String> (None until mapped? For dry-run, unmapped → skipped with reason unmapped_tool; repo null), scenario, fault_class, token, decision, reason, bd_issue_id (null), row_provenance {scenario, model, tool} — but row_provenance per row... proposals are per fault key, yet row_provenance has model. Hmm: spec says each proposal row carries row_provenance (report row scenario/model/tool values). Since a proposal aggregates multiple rows, row_provenance could be the aggregated distinct models? Design.md schema example: row_provenance: {\"scenario\": \"...\", \"model\": \"...\", \"tool\": \"...\"}. With multiple models, maybe row_provenance carries distinct models list? The spec text says \"report row scenario/model/tool values\". I'll make row_provenance an array of provenance per contributing row: {\"scenario\", \"model\", \"tool\"} entries. Design example shows single object but arrays are compatible enough; tests are mine. Keep it as array of objects (models distinct). Actually simpler: row_provenance: {\"models\": [...distinct...], \"rows\": n}? Stick to spec fields: scenario, model, tool. I'll emit one provenance entry per contributing tier-2 row (deduped by model): array of {scenario, model, too